(function () {
window.AINOW = window.AINOW || {};
var __exports = AINOW['data/data-product'] = AINOW['data/data-product'] || {};
const productPrompts = [

    {"id":"prod-1","title":"RICE Feature Prioritization Framework","prompt":"You are a senior product manager with 10 years of experience at Figma, Notion, and Linear. I need to prioritize a backlog of competing feature requests for a B2B project management tool serving 8,000 paying teams. Walk me through how to apply the RICE scoring framework (Reach, Impact, Confidence, Effort) in a practical B2B SaaS context. First, explain what each component means with a concrete scoring rubric: provide a 1–3 scale with descriptions for each level of each component. Then walk through how a PM should facilitate a prioritization session with engineering and design leads: what inputs to gather beforehand, how to structure the 60-minute meeting, how to handle stakeholder disagreements where each person advocates for their own feature, and how to communicate the final priority order to teams whose requests were deprioritized without damaging trust or morale.","tags":["prioritization","roadmap","frameworks"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-2","title":"Product Vision Statement from First Principles","prompt":"You are a Chief Product Officer who has built products at Stripe and Intercom. Walk me through how to write a compelling product vision statement from scratch. Explain the difference between a vision statement, a mission statement, and a strategy — and why conflating them leads to roadmap drift. Then provide a repeatable framework for drafting a vision that is ambitious enough to be inspiring, concrete enough to guide trade-off decisions, and stable enough to survive two to three years of market changes. Include a fill-in-the-blank template with three variations: one for a B2B SaaS product, one for a consumer app, and one for a developer tool. For each variation, write a fully worked example so I can see what 'good' looks like before I write my own.","tags":["strategy","vision","leadership"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:05:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-3","title":"Strategic Moat Identification for SaaS Products","prompt":"You are a product strategist who advises Series B SaaS companies. I want to rigorously analyze the strategic defensibility of a B2B SaaS product. Walk me through the six types of durable moats that matter in software: network effects (direct and indirect), switching costs (data, workflow, and contractual), economies of scale, proprietary data advantages, brand trust, and regulatory capture. For each moat type, explain what it looks like in practice for a SaaS product, provide a diagnostic question a PM can ask to measure its current strength on a 1–5 scale, and describe one concrete product decision that would strengthen it over the next 12 months. End with a prioritization framework for deciding which moat type to invest in given a product's current stage and competitive environment.","tags":["strategy","competitive","moats"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-4","title":"Now / Next / Later Roadmap from Messy Backlog","prompt":"You are a principal PM who specializes in turning chaotic backlogs into coherent strategy. I have a product backlog with over 60 items that includes customer requests, technical debt, internal asks, and exploratory ideas — all mixed together with no clear ownership or priority. Walk me through a step-by-step process to transform this into a clean Now / Next / Later roadmap. Explain how to first categorize items by type, how to align each cluster to a single strategic objective (for example, reducing churn or accelerating onboarding), and how to make principled decisions about what goes into each time horizon. Include guidance on how to handle items you are deliberately not building: how to say no clearly and track those decisions so they can be revisited without relitigating the same debates at every planning cycle.","tags":["roadmap","planning","backlog"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-5","title":"Competitive Landscape Analysis for a New Market Entry","prompt":"You are a senior product strategist preparing to enter a new market vertical. Walk me through a rigorous competitive analysis process that goes beyond feature comparison tables. Explain how to map the competitive landscape along two dimensions that actually predict user switching behavior (not just functionality). Describe how to identify the category leader's 'innovator's dilemma' — the lock-in decisions they made early that now prevent them from serving underserved segments. Provide a framework for finding the specific user segment and use case where you can win despite being smaller and newer. Include the five research methods you would use, the specific questions you would ask in customer interviews, and how you would synthesize findings into a positioning decision: where to differentiate, where to match, and where to deliberately not compete.","tags":["competitive","strategy","positioning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:20:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-6","title":"North Star Metric Selection and Alignment","prompt":"You are a growth-focused CPO who has scaled two products past 1 million active users. I need a rigorous framework for selecting and validating a North Star Metric (NSM) for a B2B SaaS product. Walk me through the three criteria a true NSM must satisfy: it must capture the value customers receive, it must be a leading indicator of long-term revenue health, and it must be something every team can influence with their work. Explain the common traps — vanity metrics, lagging indicators, and metrics that optimize one part of the funnel at the expense of another. Then provide a diagnostic process: give me five candidate metric types (for example, weekly active projects, seats used per account, integrations activated) and walk me through how to evaluate each one against the three criteria. End with guidance on how to introduce a new NSM to the organization, get buy-in from finance, sales, and engineering, and build a measurement system around it.","tags":["metrics","analytics","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-7","title":"Build vs Buy vs Partner Decision Framework","prompt":"You are a VP of Product who has navigated build-buy-partner decisions at mid-market SaaS companies. I need a repeatable decision framework for evaluating whether to build a capability in-house, buy a third-party solution, or pursue a partnership or integration. Walk me through the six dimensions of this decision: strategic differentiation, time-to-market, total cost of ownership over three years, vendor lock-in risk, data ownership and privacy implications, and engineering opportunity cost. For each dimension, provide a scoring rubric. Then explain how to weight these dimensions differently depending on whether the capability is core to the product's value proposition or purely infrastructural. Include a worked example — for instance, deciding whether to build an in-app notification system, license one, or integrate with a third-party provider — showing how the framework produces a recommendation and how to present that recommendation to a skeptical CTO.","tags":["strategy","decision-making","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-8","title":"Product-Market Fit Diagnostic","prompt":"You are a product coach who has helped 20 early-stage companies find or lose product-market fit. Walk me through a comprehensive PMF diagnostic for a B2B SaaS product that has been live for eight months. Cover three layers of evidence: quantitative signals (retention curves, activation rates, expansion revenue), qualitative signals (what customers say unprompted, how they describe the product to colleagues), and behavioral signals (which user segments return without prompting, which features they use without being taught). For each layer, provide the specific data I should collect, the thresholds that indicate strong versus weak PMF, and the most common misinterpretations (for example, high logo retention masking low seat utilization). End with a decision tree: given a specific combination of signals, what should the product team do — stay the course, pivot the segment, pivot the use case, or fundamentally rethink the value proposition?","tags":["pmf","strategy","growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-9","title":"Anti-Vision: What Your Product Will Never Become","prompt":"You are a product strategist who believes that what a product refuses to become is as important as what it aspires to be. I want to create an Anti-Vision document — a clear, principled statement of the things our product will never do, markets it will never enter, and UX patterns it will never adopt, regardless of short-term revenue pressure or customer requests. Walk me through how to facilitate a two-hour leadership workshop to produce this document. Explain how to distinguish genuine strategic constraints from temporary limitations, how to handle disagreements where different leaders have different instincts, and how to write principles that are specific enough to actually guide future decisions without being so restrictive they prevent reasonable adaptation. Include five example anti-vision statements from well-known products (for example, a note-taking app that refuses to become a project management tool) and analyze what made each one credible and strategically sound.","tags":["strategy","vision","leadership"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-10","title":"MVP Scope Definition: From Idea to Launchable","prompt":"You are a startup advisor who has scoped over 50 MVPs across consumer, B2B, and marketplace products. I have a new product idea and I need a rigorous process for defining what the MVP must include versus what is a 'fast follow' versus what is a future horizon feature. Walk me through the Functional Core method: how to identify the one job the product must do better than any alternative, how to strip every other requirement down to the question 'does this affect the core job or is it polish,' and how to make the final scope decision when engineers push for more completeness and designers push for more polish. Include a prioritization filter with five pass-fail questions each feature must answer before it gets into the MVP. End with guidance on how to document the MVP scope in a way that prevents scope creep during development — what to write down, where to keep it, and how to use it in sprint planning disagreements.","tags":["mvp","startup","scoping"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-11","title":"Quarterly Roadmap Planning Process","prompt":"You are a Director of Product Management who runs quarterly roadmap planning across three product teams and 22 engineers. Walk me through the complete quarterly planning process from kickoff to final roadmap publication. Cover the four weeks of preparation: the data review in week one (which metrics, which customer interviews, which market signals), the internal alignment meetings in week two (how to run the cross-functional review without it becoming a wishlist session), the draft roadmap review in week three (how to pressure-test assumptions and handle push-back from sales and marketing), and the final commit in week four (what 'committed' versus 'exploratory' means and how to communicate each to stakeholders). Include specific facilitation techniques for the most common failure modes: HiPPO override, sales urgency crowding out strategic work, and engineering capacity underestimation.","tags":["roadmap","planning","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-12","title":"Roadmap Communication for Different Audiences","prompt":"You are a CPO who presents roadmap updates to a board of directors, an all-hands meeting, a sales team, and an engineering team — all in the same week. Walk me through how to adapt the same underlying roadmap into four distinct communication artifacts. For the board, explain what level of detail and what framing (strategic outcomes, not feature lists) is appropriate. For the all-hands, explain how to communicate priorities without over-committing to dates or creating the wrong expectations. For the sales team, explain how to give them enough confidence to sell without turning the roadmap into a binding contract. For engineering, explain what they need beyond the feature list — context on the 'why,' constraints, and explicit guidance on what decisions they own. Include a template for each artifact and the three most common mistakes PMs make when trying to use one artifact for all audiences.","tags":["roadmap","communication","stakeholders"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-13","title":"Opportunity Sizing for Roadmap Decisions","prompt":"You are a data-informed PM who uses opportunity sizing to justify roadmap decisions to skeptical CFOs and engineering leaders. Walk me through a practical opportunity sizing process that does not require a PhD in statistics. Explain how to estimate the addressable user population for a feature, how to model the expected lift to a key metric using comparable features or analogous products as proxies, and how to translate that lift into a revenue or retention impact. Include guidance on how to handle uncertainty — when it is appropriate to use ranges instead of point estimates, how to make the uncertainty visible without undermining confidence in the analysis, and how to update the estimate as you gather real data post-launch. End with a one-page template for a feature opportunity brief that a PM can complete in two hours and that will survive scrutiny in a leadership review.","tags":["roadmap","data","analysis"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-14","title":"Technical Debt on the Roadmap: Making the Case","prompt":"You are a product manager who has successfully advocated for technical debt work on a roadmap owned by a business-focused leadership team. Walk me through how to make the business case for dedicating 20 to 30 percent of engineering capacity to technical debt each quarter. Explain how to quantify the cost of debt in terms that resonate with non-technical leaders: slower feature velocity, increased incident rate, higher engineer turnover, and compounding maintenance burden. Provide a framework for categorizing debt by urgency and impact, so you can sequence debt work alongside feature work without losing the narrative thread. Include a script for the conversation with a CEO who says 'we can refactor after we hit the growth target' — how to respond without being dismissive of the business pressure while still making the case for why waiting makes the problem worse.","tags":["roadmap","technical","leadership"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-15","title":"Dependency Mapping Across Product Teams","prompt":"You are a program manager at a company with four product teams whose roadmaps have significant interdependencies. I need to build a shared dependency map so that roadmap commitments are realistic and cross-team blockers are visible before they become launch delays. Walk me through the dependency mapping process: how to identify shared infrastructure, shared data models, shared APIs, and shared design system components that multiple teams depend on. Explain how to run the cross-team dependency workshop, what artifacts to produce, and how to assign ownership for each shared dependency. Include guidance on how to build a lightweight coordination mechanism — not a heavyweight program management process — that keeps dependencies visible without adding bureaucratic overhead. End with the five signals that indicate your dependency management process is working versus the five signals that it is breaking down.","tags":["roadmap","planning","teams"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-16","title":"Communicating a Roadmap Change to Customers","prompt":"You are a B2B product manager who needs to communicate a significant roadmap change — a feature that was publicly committed to will be delayed by two quarters — to enterprise customers who have built their own plans around your commitment. Walk me through how to handle this situation. Explain how to prepare before the communication: what facts to gather, how to understand the full scope of customer impact, and what you can offer as alternatives or mitigation. Provide a framework for the customer communication itself: the order in which to share information, how to acknowledge the impact without excessive apology that undermines confidence, and how to be specific about the new timeline in a way that rebuilds trust rather than creating another commitment you might miss. Include a template for the written communication and a script for the follow-up customer call.","tags":["communication","customers","roadmap"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-17","title":"Feature Sunset and Deprecation Plan","prompt":"You are a product manager at a SaaS company preparing to sunset a feature that 12 percent of your user base still actively uses. Walk me through the complete deprecation process from the internal decision to the final removal. Cover the four phases: the usage analysis phase (who uses it, how often, for what jobs, and what alternatives exist), the internal alignment phase (getting engineering, legal, customer success, and sales aligned before communicating externally), the customer communication phase (the timing, channels, messaging, and how to handle customers who push back or escalate), and the post-removal phase (how to monitor for unexpected impact and what to do if the removal causes more disruption than anticipated). Include a 60-day communication calendar template and the three most common mistakes companies make when sunsetting features.","tags":["roadmap","communication","deprecation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-18","title":"Horizon Planning: Now, Near, Far Strategy","prompt":"You are a VP of Product who uses a three-horizon planning model to balance execution with innovation. Walk me through how to apply the McKinsey Three Horizons model to a product team's annual planning process. Explain what belongs in each horizon — Horizon 1 (core business defense and optimization), Horizon 2 (emerging opportunities that could be significant in two to three years), and Horizon 3 (exploratory bets on five-year trends) — with examples from a B2B SaaS context. Provide guidance on how to allocate team capacity across horizons without starving the core business or killing innovation. Include the governance model: how often each horizon is reviewed, who has decision authority in each, and how an initiative moves from Horizon 3 to Horizon 2 to Horizon 1. End with the most common failure mode: companies that collapse all three horizons into Horizon 1 and lose the ability to innovate.","tags":["strategy","planning","innovation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-19","title":"Jobs-to-Be-Done Discovery Interview Guide","prompt":"You are a user researcher who specializes in the Jobs-to-Be-Done framework and has conducted over 300 switching interviews. I need to design a JTBD discovery interview protocol for a B2B productivity tool. Walk me through the complete interview design: the recruitment criteria for finding participants who have recently made a relevant purchase or switching decision, the warm-up questions that build rapport and establish the timeline of events, the core narrative questions that uncover the functional, emotional, and social jobs the product is hired to do, and the probing questions that reveal the forces driving and resisting change. Explain the 'timeline of events' technique in detail — how to trace the customer's journey backward from the moment of purchase to the first moment they felt the need. Provide a 45-minute interview script with facilitator notes, including how to handle participants who want to talk about features instead of the underlying jobs.","tags":["research","jtbd","interviews"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:30:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-20","title":"User Persona Development from Research Data","prompt":"You are a UX researcher who builds evidence-based personas rather than demographic archetypes. I have conducted 18 user interviews and have 200 survey responses. Walk me through the process of synthesizing this data into two or three actionable personas that will actually influence product decisions. Explain how to use affinity mapping to cluster behaviors and goals rather than demographics, how to identify the 'anchor' quote that makes each persona feel real and memorable to the team, and how to avoid the two most common persona failures: making them too generic to guide decisions, or making them so specific they do not generalize to a meaningful user population. Include a persona template with the fields that actually matter for product decisions: the primary job to be done, the current workaround, the trigger event that creates urgency, the evaluation criteria, and the success metric from the user's perspective.","tags":["research","personas","ux"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-21","title":"Moderated Usability Test Script","prompt":"You are a senior UX researcher designing a moderated usability test for a newly redesigned onboarding flow for a project management tool. Write a complete 60-minute usability test script for five participants. Include: the facilitator introduction script (the exact words to say about think-aloud protocol, recording consent, and the fact that you are testing the product not the participant), the warm-up questions about current tool usage, five task scenarios written in natural language without giving away the solution, probing questions to ask when participants are stuck or when they complete a task, a debrief section that asks about overall impressions and hidden frustrations, and facilitator notes about what to watch for and common facilitation mistakes like nodding too enthusiastically or rescuing participants too early. End with a scoring rubric for task completion, time on task, and error count.","tags":["research","usability","testing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-22","title":"Continuous Discovery Habits for Product Teams","prompt":"You are a product coach who trains teams in Teresa Torres's continuous discovery methodology. I want to establish a continuous discovery practice for my product team that currently does research in occasional bursts before major launches. Walk me through the operating rhythm: how to structure weekly 30-minute customer interviews with a rotating interview schedule so no single person burns out, how to use an opportunity solution tree to connect interview insights to product opportunities to potential solutions, and how to make discovery outputs visible and actionable in sprint planning. Address the most common objections: 'we do not have time,' 'we already know our customers,' and 'interviews are too slow to keep up with development.' Include a 90-day rollout plan for transitioning a team from episodic to continuous discovery, with the milestones and behaviors you would use to measure whether the habit is taking hold.","tags":["research","discovery","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:45:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-23","title":"Quantitative Survey Design for Product Research","prompt":"You are a product researcher who designs surveys that produce data product teams can actually act on. I need to design a survey to understand why users are not activating a core feature despite being shown it during onboarding. Walk me through the survey design process: how to write a screening question that identifies the right respondents, how to sequence questions to avoid priming bias, how to write close-ended questions with response scales that produce analyzable data, and how to include two open-ended questions strategically without creating an analysis burden. Include guidance on sample size — how many responses do I need to have confidence in segmentation cuts by company size and role — and how to handle the trade-off between survey length and completion rate. Provide ten example questions I can adapt, with notes on the analysis each question enables.","tags":["research","surveys","data"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-24","title":"Churn Interview Protocol","prompt":"You are a B2B product manager who has turned churn interviews into a systematic retention improvement process. I need a protocol for interviewing users who have recently cancelled or churned from our product. Walk me through how to structure the outreach (who to contact, what to say to get a 25 to 30 percent response rate from churned users who have no incentive to talk to you), the interview framework (the specific questions that reveal whether churn was caused by product gaps, pricing misalignment, competitive switching, or life events outside your control), and the analysis process (how to code responses and identify patterns across 20 or more interviews). Include the five most revealing questions to ask a churned customer, with the follow-up probes for each. End with a template for a monthly churn insight summary that connects interview findings to specific retention hypotheses the product team can test.","tags":["research","churn","retention"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-25","title":"User Research Synthesis and Insight Generation","prompt":"You are a UX researcher who specializes in synthesis — the hardest and most undervalued part of the research process. I have just completed eight user interviews and I have 14 pages of notes. Walk me through your complete synthesis process from raw notes to actionable insights. Cover the three levels of synthesis: observations (what you saw and heard without interpretation), patterns (observations that appear across multiple participants), and insights (the 'so what' — what the pattern means for the product). Explain how to use affinity mapping to move from observations to patterns, how to write a good insight statement (the difference between 'users are confused by the navigation' and a proper insight), and how to prioritize insights by their potential impact on the product. Include a template for a research readout that communicates insights to a product team in a way that drives decisions rather than creating a document no one reads.","tags":["research","synthesis","ux"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-26","title":"Customer Advisory Board Setup and Facilitation","prompt":"You are a B2B product leader who has run Customer Advisory Boards at two SaaS companies. I want to set up a CAB for the first time. Walk me through the complete setup: how to select the right eight to twelve members (the mix of company sizes, roles, tenure, and usage patterns that produces the most useful input), how to structure the charter and expectations (what members commit to, what they get in return, what confidentiality obligations apply), and how to design the first meeting agenda so it builds trust and establishes productive norms rather than turning into a feature request session. Include facilitation techniques for the most common CAB failure modes: members who dominate the discussion, members who attend but disengage, and leadership teams that ignore CAB input after investing in the relationship. Provide a 90-minute meeting agenda template and a post-meeting synthesis process.","tags":["research","customers","leadership"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-27","title":"Net Promoter Score Deep-Dive Analysis","prompt":"You are a data-driven product manager who uses NPS as a leading indicator for retention, not just a vanity metric. I have an overall NPS of 34 with 600 responses and I want to turn this into actionable product decisions. Walk me through the analysis process that goes beyond the headline score. Explain how to segment NPS by customer tenure, plan tier, company size, and primary use case to identify which segments are strongest and weakest. Describe how to analyze the open-text responses at scale — the coding taxonomy, the priority matrix, and how to connect qualitative themes to quantitative score differences. Include guidance on how to design NPS follow-up surveys for Detractors (what questions uncover fixable product gaps versus fundamental fit issues) and Promoters (what questions identify referral opportunities and moments worth amplifying). End with a 30-60-90 day action plan for improving NPS by 8 to 10 points based on a hypothetical set of findings.","tags":["metrics","nps","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-28","title":"Diary Study Protocol for Longitudinal Research","prompt":"You are a UX researcher designing a diary study to understand how users integrate a new productivity tool into their existing workflows over four weeks. Walk me through the complete diary study design: participant recruitment criteria and sample size (how many is enough for a longitudinal study given the higher dropout risk), the prompting cadence and question design (daily versus weekly prompts, structured versus open-ended entries, how to keep participants engaged over the full study period), the analysis approach for longitudinal qualitative data (how to track individual trajectories versus aggregate patterns), and how to supplement diary entries with two check-in interviews per participant to probe emerging themes. Include the exact prompt language for daily diary entries, an engagement maintenance strategy (what to do when a participant goes silent for three days), and a synthesis framework for producing insights about how behavior changes over time rather than at a single snapshot.","tags":["research","qualitative","longitudinal"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-29","title":"Onboarding Flow Design: First 10 Minutes","prompt":"You are a product designer who has optimized onboarding flows for SaaS products and improved 30-day retention by designing the first ten minutes of the user experience. Walk me through the design principles and decision framework for onboarding. Cover the three jobs the onboarding flow must accomplish simultaneously: getting the user to their first meaningful value moment as fast as possible, collecting the setup information the product genuinely needs to be useful, and establishing the behavioral habits that correlate with long-term retention. Explain how to diagnose an existing onboarding flow for friction using funnel analytics and session recordings. Provide a step-by-step design process for a B2B SaaS onboarding flow, from the post-signup landing screen through the first 'aha' moment. Include specific patterns: when to use a guided tour versus a task-based checklist versus contextual tooltips, and how to handle the cold-start problem when the product needs data to be useful but has none yet.","tags":["ux","onboarding","design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:20:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-30","title":"Information Architecture for Complex B2B Products","prompt":"You are a senior UX designer who specializes in information architecture for enterprise software with hundreds of features and multiple user roles. I need to design the IA for a product that currently has a flat navigation that is collapsing under the weight of new features. Walk me through the IA design process: how to conduct a card sorting study to understand how users mentally group features, how to run a tree testing exercise to validate that users can find things in the proposed hierarchy, and how to design a navigation structure that serves both novice users who need progressive disclosure and power users who want direct access. Include specific guidance on how to handle role-based access — when the IA should present different structures to different user roles versus when you should use a single structure with contextual visibility. Provide a checklist for evaluating whether a proposed IA is ready for development.","tags":["ux","ia","navigation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-31","title":"Design System Foundation: Building the Right Primitives","prompt":"You are a design systems lead who has built design systems from scratch at two SaaS companies. I need to build the foundation of a design system for a product team of four designers and twelve engineers. Walk me through what to prioritize in the first 90 days. Cover the decision of what goes in the design system versus what stays in the product (not everything belongs in the system), how to design the token layer (color, typography, spacing, and motion tokens) with semantic naming that survives a rebrand, how to choose the right component primitives to build first (the 20 percent of components that appear in 80 percent of UI), and how to establish contribution and governance processes so the system does not become a bottleneck. Include specific guidance on the design system documentation format that engineers actually use, and the metrics you would track to measure whether the design system is achieving its goals.","tags":["design-system","ux","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-32","title":"Empty States, Error States, and Loading States","prompt":"You are a product designer who believes that the non-ideal states — empty, error, loading — are where product quality is actually determined. Walk me through the design strategy for all three state types in a B2B dashboard product. For empty states, explain the four types (first-time empty, user-cleared empty, no-results empty, and error-caused empty) and the different design response each requires, including how to use empty states as onboarding opportunities without being patronizing. For error states, explain the hierarchy from inline validation errors to page-level errors to system-wide outages, the writing principles for error messages that help users recover rather than just describing what went wrong, and how to design graceful degradation when partial data is available. For loading states, explain when to use skeletons versus spinners versus optimistic UI, and how to set accurate loading expectations without making the UI feel slower than it is.","tags":["ux","design","states"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-33","title":"Mobile-First UX Design Principles","prompt":"You are a mobile UX designer who has shipped products used by tens of millions of people on mobile devices. Walk me through the design principles and decision frameworks specific to mobile-first product design. Cover the ergonomics layer: thumb zone analysis, minimum touch target sizes, and how to design for one-handed use on large screens. Cover the context layer: how mobile usage contexts (interrupted, distracted, on the move) should change interaction patterns compared to desktop equivalents. Cover the technical layer: how network constraints and device performance variability affect interaction design decisions. Provide specific guidance on navigation patterns — when tab bars, hamburger menus, bottom sheets, and gesture-based navigation are each appropriate — and the trade-offs between discoverability and efficiency at different stages of user familiarity with the product.","tags":["ux","mobile","design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-34","title":"WCAG 2.1 Accessibility Audit Process","prompt":"You are an accessibility specialist who conducts WCAG 2.1 AA audits for SaaS products. Walk me through a structured accessibility audit process that a product team can run internally without a specialized tool. Cover the four WCAG principles (Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust) and the specific checks that catch the highest-frequency failures in web products: color contrast ratios for text and interactive elements, keyboard navigability and focus management, screen reader compatibility for dynamic content and form validation, and accessible names for all interactive elements. Provide a prioritized audit checklist with 25 items ordered by frequency of failure and severity of impact. For each item, include the WCAG success criterion reference, a practical test method, and an example of a compliant and a non-compliant implementation. End with guidance on how to integrate accessibility testing into the design review and QA process so issues are caught before they reach production.","tags":["accessibility","ux","compliance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-35","title":"UX Writing Principles for Product Interfaces","prompt":"You are a UX writer who has defined voice and tone guidelines for products used by millions of people. Walk me through the principles of effective UX writing with practical examples for each. Cover the four core principles: clarity (writing that eliminates ambiguity about what will happen), conciseness (removing words that do not add meaning), usefulness (writing that helps users accomplish their goal rather than describing the system), and human warmth (writing that feels like a person rather than a machine). For each principle, provide three before-and-after examples from real interface contexts: button labels, error messages, empty states, confirmation dialogs, and onboarding copy. Include a style guide starter template with sections on voice and tone, active versus passive voice, number and date formatting, capitalization, and how to write for international audiences who read in translation.","tags":["ux-writing","design","communication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-36","title":"Dashboard and Data Visualization Design","prompt":"You are a product designer who specializes in data-dense dashboards for B2B analytics products. I need to design a dashboard for operations managers who monitor real-time KPIs, investigate anomalies, and generate reports for weekly stakeholder meetings. Walk me through the design process from user needs to visual hierarchy. Cover how to conduct a 'data audit' — identifying which metrics users actually act on versus which ones they look at out of habit — and how to use this to determine what belongs above the fold. Explain the visual hierarchy principles specific to dashboards: how to use size, color, and position to guide attention to the most important signals, when to use charts versus numbers versus status indicators, and how to design for both quick scanning and deep investigation in the same view. Include specific guidance on designing for colorblindness, high-density data environments, and the transition between summary and detail views.","tags":["ux","dashboard","data"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-37","title":"Micro-Interaction Design for Quality and Delight","prompt":"You are a product designer who believes micro-interactions are the difference between a product that feels cheap and one that feels crafted. Walk me through the design strategy for micro-interactions in a B2B SaaS application. Explain Dan Saffer's four-part micro-interaction model: trigger, rules, feedback, and loops and modes. For each part, provide three to five concrete examples from common SaaS UI patterns — form submission, data save confirmation, drag-and-drop reordering, real-time collaboration presence, and notification delivery. Cover the engineering collaboration aspect: how to write motion design specs that engineers can implement faithfully, what information to include (duration, easing curve, trigger condition, and accessibility behavior), and when animation is inappropriate — situations where it adds cognitive load or slows perceived performance. Include a micro-interaction audit checklist for reviewing an existing product and identifying the five highest-impact opportunities.","tags":["ux","animation","design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-38","title":"Form Design Best Practices for Complex Data Entry","prompt":"You are a UX designer who has optimized form completion rates for enterprise software where users enter complex structured data. Walk me through the design principles for forms that users complete accurately and efficiently. Cover the structural decisions: single-column versus multi-column layout, field ordering (the sequence that matches users' mental model versus the sequence that matches the database), and when to break a long form into a multi-step wizard versus keeping it on a single page. Cover the validation strategy: the trade-off between real-time validation and on-submit validation, how to write validation messages that explain what to do rather than what went wrong, and how to handle asynchronous validation for fields like username availability. Include guidance on the hardest form design challenges: dependent fields (where the available options in one field depend on the selection in another), optional versus required field conventions, and designing forms that work equally well for keyboard-only users.","tags":["ux","forms","design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-39","title":"Design Review Process and Critique Framework","prompt":"You are a design lead who has built critique and review processes at design teams ranging from two to twenty designers. I want to establish a structured design review process for my product team that improves design quality without creating a bottleneck or making the process feel like a judgment exercise. Walk me through how to design the review cadence (when in the design process reviews happen, who attends each stage of review), the critique framework (how to give feedback that is specific, actionable, and grounded in user needs rather than personal preference), and the documentation process (how to capture decisions and the reasoning behind them so they do not need to be relitigated). Include specific language for common critique situations: how to push back on a design decision made by a senior stakeholder, how to give feedback to a designer whose confidence is fragile, and how to handle the PM who keeps asking for design changes after the review is closed.","tags":["design","process","teams"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-40","title":"User Story Writing That Engineers Can Build From","prompt":"You are a senior product manager who writes user stories that engineering teams consistently praise for clarity and completeness. Walk me through how to write user stories that are genuinely useful rather than formulaic. Explain when the standard 'as a user, I want to, so that' format is helpful and when it obscures important context. Cover the five components of a strong user story: the actor (which specific user type, not 'a user'), the action (what they are trying to accomplish, not which button to click), the motivation (the actual goal behind the action), the acceptance criteria (the specific observable outcomes that confirm the story is done), and the context (edge cases, performance expectations, and what happens when things go wrong). Include five worked examples across different feature types — a search feature, a notification setting, a data export, a permission change, and a dashboard widget — showing mediocre stories transformed into excellent ones.","tags":["agile","stories","requirements"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-41","title":"Product Requirements Document Template","prompt":"You are a staff PM who writes PRDs that save weeks of alignment time by anticipating every question engineering, design, legal, and data teams will ask before the project starts. Walk me through the anatomy of a great PRD. Cover the sections that every PRD needs: the problem statement (what user pain this solves and why now), the goals and non-goals (what success looks like and what you are explicitly not trying to do with this project), the user stories with acceptance criteria, the functional requirements, the non-functional requirements (performance, security, accessibility, and internationalization), the analytics events to instrument, the rollout and flag strategy, and the open questions section that tracks unresolved decisions. For each section, explain what information is truly necessary versus what PMs pad out of habit. Include a one-page PRD lite template for small features and a full PRD template for large initiatives, with guidance on when each is appropriate.","tags":["requirements","prd","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:20:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-42","title":"Acceptance Criteria That Prevent Launch Day Surprises","prompt":"You are a QA-focused product manager who has seen too many launches derailed by acceptance criteria that were too vague to catch edge cases before deployment. Walk me through how to write acceptance criteria that give the engineering team enough specificity to build correctly and give QA enough specificity to test thoroughly. Explain the difference between scenario-based acceptance criteria (Given-When-Then format) and checklist-style criteria, and when each is appropriate. Provide a taxonomy of acceptance criteria types: happy path, error path, boundary conditions, performance expectations, security requirements, and accessibility requirements. For a specific feature — a team member invitation flow — write a complete set of acceptance criteria covering all six types, showing the level of specificity that prevents the most common build-test-ship failures. End with a review checklist: the questions a PM should ask before signing off on a story's acceptance criteria.","tags":["requirements","quality","agile"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-43","title":"Story Mapping for a Complete User Journey","prompt":"You are a product manager who uses story mapping to align teams on what to build and in what order. I need to build a story map for a new feature: a self-serve analytics reporting module for a B2B SaaS product. Walk me through the story mapping process step by step. Explain how to structure the backbone (the high-level user activities that form the top row of the map), how to decompose each activity into user tasks (the second row), and how to slice the tasks into releases horizontally (identifying the minimum viable release that delivers end-to-end value). Include guidance on how to facilitate the story mapping workshop with a cross-functional team — the materials needed, the facilitation moves to keep the group focused on user activities rather than implementation details, and how to handle the team members who want to jump straight to technical architecture. Provide a complete example story map for the reporting module scenario.","tags":["agile","planning","stories"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-44","title":"Edge Case and Exception Handling Specification","prompt":"You are a product manager at a fintech company who has learned from experience that edge cases are not edge cases — they are the cases that determine whether enterprise customers trust the product. Walk me through how to systematically identify and specify edge cases before development begins. Provide a taxonomy of edge case categories: concurrency conflicts (two users acting on the same data simultaneously), boundary conditions (zero items, maximum items, first item, last item), permission edge cases (actions taken at the moment permissions change), connectivity failures (actions taken when the network is intermittent), and data integrity issues (missing required data, malformed imports, duplicate detection). For each category, explain a discovery technique — the question or scenario you would present to the team to surface the cases they had not thought about. Include a template for documenting edge cases in a format that is useful for both development and QA.","tags":["requirements","quality","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-45","title":"API Product Requirements for Developer Experience","prompt":"You are a product manager who specializes in API products and developer experience. I need to write requirements for a public REST API that will be used by third-party developers to integrate our data into their own products. Walk me through the requirements process specific to API products. Cover the developer experience requirements that are often treated as optional but are actually core product quality: consistent naming conventions, predictable error response formats, comprehensive rate limiting with clear feedback, versioning and deprecation policy, idempotency for mutating operations, and pagination for collection endpoints. Explain how to write API requirements that are specific enough to prevent inconsistent implementations across different endpoints built by different engineers. Include a requirements template for a single API endpoint that covers all these dimensions, using a concrete example like a 'create project' endpoint for a project management tool.","tags":["api","requirements","developer"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-46","title":"Localization and Internationalization Requirements","prompt":"You are a global product manager who has shipped products in 12 languages across 25 countries. I need to write i18n and l10n requirements for a product that is currently English-only and is preparing to expand to Spanish, French, German, and Japanese. Walk me through the full scope of internationalization requirements that are often underestimated: text expansion (German text is typically 30 percent longer than English, what does this do to UI components designed for English strings), right-to-left language support preparation, date and time formatting by locale, number and currency formatting, address format variation by country, character encoding requirements, and the pluralization rules that differ significantly across languages. Provide a requirements checklist organized by component type (forms, notifications, error messages, date pickers, navigation) with specific implementation notes for the languages in scope. Include a testing matrix for validating i18n compliance before each language goes live.","tags":["requirements","global","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-47","title":"Privacy and Data Requirements for Product Features","prompt":"You are a product manager at a privacy-first SaaS company working alongside a legal team on GDPR and CCPA compliance. I need to write data privacy requirements for a new feature that collects and processes user behavioral data for product analytics. Walk me through the privacy requirements process from a product perspective. Cover the data minimization principle (collecting only what is genuinely needed), the lawful basis determination (consent versus legitimate interest), the retention and deletion requirements (how long data is kept and what the deletion mechanism is), the data subject rights implementation (how users can access, correct, export, and delete their data), the third-party data sharing disclosure requirements, and the consent management UX. For each requirement area, explain the product manifestation — the actual UI components, settings, and flows that give the requirement a form users can interact with. Include a data inventory template for tracking all data collected by a feature.","tags":["requirements","privacy","compliance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-48","title":"Activation Metric Design and Measurement","prompt":"You are a product analyst who has defined activation metrics for B2B SaaS products at companies ranging from seed stage to Series D. I need to define an activation metric for a project management tool where we know that users who invite two teammates and create three projects in their first week have dramatically higher 30-day retention, but our current activation definition just measures whether the user logs in three times in the first seven days. Walk me through the process of defining a better activation metric. Explain how to use retention cohort analysis to identify which early behaviors are genuinely predictive of long-term retention (the methodology, the data required, and how to distinguish correlation from causation). Cover how to define an activation event that is specific, measurable, and achievable in the first session or first week, how to validate the definition with an A/B test, and how to build a monitoring system that surfaces activation rate by cohort and by acquisition channel.","tags":["metrics","activation","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-49","title":"Retention Cohort Analysis and Interpretation","prompt":"You are a data-informed PM who uses retention cohort analysis as a primary diagnostic tool. Walk me through how to set up, analyze, and act on retention cohort data for a B2B SaaS product. Explain the difference between customer retention (logo retention), user retention (seat or account retention), and revenue retention (net dollar retention) and why a product team needs to track all three separately. Cover how to build a cohort retention table in SQL or a BI tool, how to read the resulting table to identify the natural retention level (where the curve flattens), which cohort comparisons reveal the impact of product changes, and how to use segment-level retention analysis to identify your best and worst user segments. Include specific interpretation guidance: what a retention curve that never flattens means, what a step-change improvement in a specific cohort's curve tells you, and how to translate retention curve shape into product decisions.","tags":["metrics","retention","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-50","title":"Product Metrics Dashboard Design","prompt":"You are a product operations lead who designs metrics dashboards that product teams actually use to make decisions. I need to design a weekly metrics dashboard for a product team of three PMs, four designers, and twelve engineers working on a B2B SaaS product. Walk me through the dashboard design process. Cover how to select the right metrics for the team level (the team's three to five core metrics versus the company-level metrics and the individual feature metrics), how to structure the dashboard to show the health signal clearly (what is trending, what is anomalous, what needs investigation), and how to design the review cadence (the weekly ritual that ensures the team actually looks at the data and discusses it). Include specific guidance on the metrics that are most commonly included but provide the least signal (daily active users for a B2B tool, for example), and the metrics that are commonly missing but most predictive of future performance.","tags":["metrics","analytics","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-51","title":"Instrumentation Plan for a New Feature","prompt":"You are a product manager who writes analytics instrumentation plans that data engineers can implement without ambiguity. I need to write an instrumentation plan for a new document collaboration feature. Walk me through the complete instrumentation planning process: how to identify the user behaviors and product moments that need to be tracked (the events taxonomy), how to define each event with precision (event name, properties, trigger condition, and example payload), how to design the event naming convention so the analytics schema remains queryable as the product grows, and how to prioritize events when engineering time is limited. Include a complete instrumentation plan for the document collaboration feature with 15 to 20 events covering the full user journey from document creation through sharing, editing, and commenting. Provide a template for event definition that includes all the fields needed for clean implementation.","tags":["metrics","analytics","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-52","title":"Customer Health Score Design","prompt":"You are a product manager at a B2B SaaS company who is designing a customer health score to help the customer success team prioritize their proactive outreach. Walk me through the health score design process. Cover the input selection — which product usage signals are most predictive of renewal and expansion (login frequency, feature adoption breadth, API usage, team size growth, support ticket volume), how to weight each input based on its predictive power, and how to handle the cold-start problem for new customers who do not yet have enough history. Explain how to validate the health score model by back-testing against historical churn data, how to set thresholds that trigger CS alerts, and how to communicate the score to CS teams so they understand what is driving it rather than treating it as a black box. Include a scoring rubric that produces a 0 to 100 score and a playbook for the three CS interventions triggered by different score ranges.","tags":["metrics","retention","customer-success"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-53","title":"Pirate Metrics (AARRR) Audit for SaaS","prompt":"You are a growth-focused product manager who uses the AARRR framework (Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Referral, Revenue) as a diagnostic tool rather than a reporting framework. Walk me through how to conduct a full AARRR audit for a B2B SaaS product that has been live for 18 months. For each stage, explain the most meaningful metric to track (not the most commonly tracked), how to benchmark it against industry norms, and the most common root causes when the metric is underperforming. Cover the stage-to-stage conversion analysis: how to calculate conversion rates between each funnel stage, identify which transition has the worst conversion, and prioritize improvement efforts based on the compounding impact of fixing an early funnel stage versus a late one. End with a 90-day improvement plan structure: how to sequence experiments across funnel stages to maximize overall impact given a constraint of one engineering sprint per experiment.","tags":["metrics","growth","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-54","title":"Experiment Velocity and Learning Rate Optimization","prompt":"You are a product leader who has built high-velocity experimentation cultures at two growth-stage companies. I want to increase my team's experiment velocity from two experiments per month to eight experiments per month without sacrificing rigor. Walk me through the process. Cover the operational constraints that limit experiment velocity (engineering setup time, minimum sample size requirements, analysis bottlenecks, and decision-making latency) and the specific interventions for each bottleneck. Explain how to build an experiment framework that reduces the setup time for a new A/B test from five days to one day, how to use sequential testing methods to reach valid conclusions faster than fixed-horizon tests allow, and how to build a decision log that ensures experiment results are acted on quickly. Include the cultural changes needed: how to build a team norm around 'learning rate' rather than 'win rate,' and how to celebrate informative null results as valuable as wins.","tags":["experimentation","growth","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-55","title":"Revenue Metrics Beyond MRR for SaaS","prompt":"You are a SaaS CFO-aligned product manager who tracks the revenue metrics that predict the long-term health of the business. Walk me through the complete set of revenue metrics a product team should monitor and influence. Cover MRR and ARR with their components (new MRR, expansion MRR, contraction MRR, churn MRR), net dollar retention and why it is the single most important metric for a SaaS business past product-market fit, customer acquisition cost and LTV with the ratio threshold that signals a healthy business, average revenue per account and how to track its evolution as pricing strategy changes, and payback period as a capital efficiency metric. For each metric, explain the product decisions that most directly influence it — the feature investments, pricing changes, and user experience improvements that move the metric in a meaningful direction. Include a monthly revenue metrics review template that connects product activity to revenue impact.","tags":["metrics","revenue","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-56","title":"A/B Test Hypothesis and Design Framework","prompt":"You are a experimentation specialist who has designed over 200 statistically valid A/B tests. Walk me through the complete process of designing a rigorous A/B test from initial hypothesis to launch. Cover the hypothesis structure (the specific change you are making, the mechanism by which it should improve behavior, and the metric you expect to move), the experimental unit selection (user-level versus session-level versus account-level randomization and why the choice matters), the sample size calculation (how to use effect size, baseline conversion rate, and statistical power to determine how long to run the test), the threat to validity analysis (novelty effects, network effects between treatment and control, and seasonal confounders), and the guardrail metrics (the metrics you will monitor to ensure the test is not improving one metric while degrading something important). End with a pre-mortems exercise: what are the five most likely reasons this test will produce an inconclusive or misleading result, and how do you design against each one?","tags":["testing","experimentation","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:35:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-57","title":"Interpreting A/B Test Results Without Fooling Yourself","prompt":"You are a statistician turned product manager who has seen teams misinterpret A/B test results in every possible way. Walk me through the most common statistical mistakes in A/B test interpretation and how to avoid each one. Cover p-hacking and early stopping (why checking significance every day invalidates the test), multiple comparison problems (why running ten simultaneous A/B tests dramatically increases false positive rates), the base rate fallacy (why a 20 percent lift on a conversion rate of 0.5 percent is not as exciting as it sounds), Simpson's Paradox (how aggregate results can be positive while segment-level results are negative), and the practical significance versus statistical significance distinction (why a statistically significant result can be too small to justify the maintenance cost of the change). Include a results interpretation checklist with the questions a PM should answer before shipping a winning variant. End with guidance on what to do when results are directionally positive but do not reach statistical significance.","tags":["testing","statistics","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-58","title":"Multivariate Testing Strategy","prompt":"You are a growth engineer turned PM who has run multivariate tests in high-traffic consumer products. Walk me through when to use multivariate testing (MVT) versus a sequence of A/B tests. Explain the statistical mechanics of a full factorial MVT design — how interactions between variables are measured, why the sample size requirement grows multiplicatively with the number of variants, and the conditions under which a fractional factorial design is appropriate. Cover the practical constraints: the traffic volumes required for MVT to produce valid results within a reasonable timeframe, the analysis complexity that results from interaction effects, and the implementation complexity when testing combinations of design, copy, and behavioral changes simultaneously. Provide a decision framework for choosing between a single multi-variable test and a sequential A/B testing roadmap for the same research question, with guidance on which approach is right given different traffic levels and iteration speed requirements.","tags":["testing","experimentation","growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-59","title":"Pricing A/B Test Design","prompt":"You are a pricing strategist at a SaaS company who has run pricing experiments without triggering customer backlash. I want to A/B test our pricing page to improve trial-to-paid conversion. Walk me through the specific considerations for pricing experiments that are different from standard feature experiments. Cover the ethical and legal dimensions (price discrimination laws in different jurisdictions, what disclosures are required, and what constitutes an acceptable pricing test versus a deceptive practice), the experimental design challenges (how to prevent treatment and control users from comparing prices, how to handle customers who see both variants across sessions), and the measurement complexity (how to measure not just conversion rate but also plan mix, LTV by plan, and long-term retention by price point). Include a taxonomy of pricing experiment types ordered from least to most risky — from testing page layout and copy to testing actual price levels — with guidance on when each type is appropriate given a company's maturity and customer relationship health.","tags":["testing","pricing","growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-60","title":"Feature Flag Rollout Strategy","prompt":"You are a platform engineer turned PM who designs safe rollout strategies for high-risk feature releases. Walk me through a complete feature flag rollout strategy for a major change to a core product workflow — specifically, replacing an existing file upload interface with a new drag-and-drop version. Cover the rollout stages: internal employees only, then a five percent random sample, then 25 percent, then 50 percent, then 100 percent. For each stage, explain what metrics to monitor, what success thresholds to require before proceeding to the next stage, and what the rollback trigger conditions are. Cover the user experience of partial rollouts — should users in the control group see any indication that a change is being tested, how to handle support tickets from users who are in different groups, and how to communicate the full rollout to users once it is complete. Include a rollout health dashboard specification: the five metrics that must be green before each stage advance.","tags":["testing","release","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-61","title":"Qualitative Research to Complement A/B Test Results","prompt":"You are a mixed-methods researcher who believes quantitative experiments without qualitative context lead to local optima. I just ran an A/B test where the variant improved signup conversion by 8 percent but reduced feature activation by 12 percent. The test created more users who did not become engaged users. Walk me through how to design a qualitative research program to understand the mechanism behind this result. Explain how to select research participants from the variant group who converted but did not activate, the interview protocol to understand their expectations at signup versus what they experienced in the product, and how to synthesize findings into specific hypotheses about what the signup experience promised that the product did not deliver. Provide a framework for using qualitative insight to generate the next generation of experiments that improve conversion without sacrificing activation — and for building a principle from this finding that informs future conversion optimization work.","tags":["testing","research","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-62","title":"Experimentation Roadmap and Prioritization","prompt":"You are a head of growth who runs an experimentation program with three engineers and two analysts dedicated to optimization work. I need to build a three-month experimentation roadmap that maximizes learning velocity and business impact. Walk me through how to build an opportunity inventory — the list of all the places in the product where experimentation could produce meaningful improvement — and how to score each opportunity by expected impact, confidence in the hypothesis, and engineering effort. Explain the sequencing principles: why to start with the highest-traffic, lowest-complexity experiments to build team muscle before tackling complex multi-variable tests, how to avoid test collision (multiple tests running on the same user journey), and how to balance optimization of existing flows with testing fundamentally new approaches. Provide a one-page experimentation roadmap template with columns for hypothesis, metric, expected lift, engineering effort, start date, and learning output.","tags":["testing","growth","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-63","title":"Product-Led Growth Transformation Strategy","prompt":"You are a PLG consultant who has helped sales-led companies transition to a product-led growth motion. I am a VP of Product at a mid-market B2B SaaS company where all revenue currently flows through a sales team. I need to make the case for and design the transition to a product-led motion. Walk me through the full PLG transformation strategy. Cover the business case (the unit economics of PLG versus sales-led, the evidence from comparable companies, and the risks of not transitioning), the product changes required (the freemium or free trial model design, the viral and expansion mechanics to build into the product, and the product qualification criteria that determine when a PQL is ready for sales contact), the organizational changes required (how CS, sales, and marketing roles change in a PLG motion), and the metrics to track the transition. Include a 12-month implementation roadmap with milestones, and the three things most likely to cause the transition to fail.","tags":["plg","growth","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-64","title":"Freemium Model Design","prompt":"You are a product strategist who has designed freemium models for B2B SaaS products with conversion rates significantly above industry average. Walk me through the design principles for a freemium model that converts free users to paid at a healthy rate. Cover the three dimensions of the freemium decision: feature gates (which features to include in free, which to restrict to paid, and how to design the upgrade moment so it is helpful rather than annoying), usage limits (the right ceiling for storage, seats, API calls, or projects that creates genuine value in free while creating a natural upgrade trigger), and time limits (when a time-limited trial is preferable to a perpetual free tier). Explain the core freemium design tension: free must be valuable enough that users develop habits and invite teammates, but constrained enough that it creates natural upgrade pressure. Provide a decision framework for each gate type, with examples from products that have designed each well and each poorly.","tags":["plg","pricing","growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-65","title":"Viral Loops and Referral Mechanics","prompt":"You are a growth PM who has designed viral loops that achieved K-factors above 0.4 in B2B SaaS products. Walk me through the design principles for building viral and referral mechanics into a product. Explain the difference between inherent virality (the product becomes more valuable when you invite others, as in a shared workspace), incentivized virality (a referral program with explicit rewards), and word-of-mouth virality (the product creates shareable moments or outcomes). For each type, provide two to three design patterns that work in B2B SaaS, the specific product moments where viral invitations feel natural rather than forced, and the retention dynamics that determine whether a viral loop sustains itself or decays. Include the K-factor calculation and explain what inputs are most controllable through product design. End with a viral loop audit checklist for evaluating an existing product's viral potential and identifying the highest-leverage design opportunities.","tags":["plg","viral","growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-66","title":"Product Qualified Lead (PQL) Framework","prompt":"You are a growth PM who built the PQL scoring system at a Series C SaaS company that improved sales efficiency by 40 percent. I need to define a Product Qualified Lead (PQL) framework for a project management tool with a freemium model. Walk me through the PQL framework design process. Explain how to identify the product behaviors that most strongly predict a free user's readiness to buy — the combination of breadth of feature use, team collaboration activity, and engagement frequency that creates a PQL signal. Cover how to build the PQL scoring model: the inputs, the weights, the threshold, and how to validate the model against historical conversion data. Explain how to pass PQL signals to the sales team: what information they need beyond the score (which features are being used, by how many people, for how long), how to design the sales motion for PQLs versus traditional MQLs, and how to measure whether the PQL program is improving sales efficiency.","tags":["plg","sales","growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-67","title":"Self-Serve Upgrade Flow Design","prompt":"You are a conversion-focused product designer who has optimized self-serve upgrade flows in B2B SaaS products. Walk me through the design principles and decision framework for a self-serve upgrade experience. Cover the three moments that matter most in the upgrade journey: the feature gate moment (how to present a paid-only feature in a way that communicates value without feeling like a hard sell), the upgrade intent moment (how to design the upgrade flow itself — the pricing page, the plan comparison, and the checkout experience), and the post-upgrade moment (how to ensure the user immediately experiences the value they paid for, and how to prevent post-upgrade buyer's remorse). Include specific UX patterns for each moment, with analysis of what separates high-converting upgrade experiences from frustrating ones. Provide a conversion audit checklist for evaluating an existing upgrade flow and identifying the three to five changes most likely to improve paid conversion rate.","tags":["plg","conversion","ux"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-68","title":"Expansion Revenue Through Product Design","prompt":"You are a product manager who treats net dollar retention as a primary product success metric. Walk me through the product design strategies that drive expansion revenue — the revenue growth that comes from existing customers adding seats, upgrading plans, or purchasing add-ons. Cover the three types of expansion moments: seat expansion (how to design team invitation and permission flows that make adding new users feel natural and valuable), plan upgrade (how to design the experience around plan limits so that hitting a limit becomes a positive signal of success rather than a frustrating blocker), and add-on adoption (how to surface add-ons contextually at the moment they are relevant rather than in a product catalog users never visit). Include metrics for each expansion type, the most common product design mistakes that suppress expansion revenue, and a 90-day product improvement plan for a team with net dollar retention below 100 percent.","tags":["plg","expansion","revenue"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-69","title":"Community-Led Growth Strategy","prompt":"You are a product lead who has built community-led growth motions alongside traditional PLG at two developer tool companies. I want to design a community-led growth strategy for a B2B SaaS product targeting marketing operations professionals. Walk me through the full CLG strategy design. Cover the community purpose definition (what problem the community solves for members beyond product support), the community format decision (forum, Slack, Discord, or events — and how to choose based on the communication style of the target audience), the content and programming strategy (the types of content and discussions that attract the target audience before they are product users), the product integration strategy (how community membership translates to product adoption without making the community feel like a marketing channel), and the measurement framework (how to track the community's contribution to pipeline, activation, retention, and advocacy). Include a 6-month launch plan with weekly programming milestones.","tags":["plg","community","growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-70","title":"MoSCoW Prioritization for Sprint Planning","prompt":"You are an agile product manager who uses MoSCoW (Must Have, Should Have, Could Have, Won't Have) to make sprint commitments that the team can honor. Walk me through how to apply MoSCoW effectively in a two-week sprint planning context. Explain how to define 'Must Have' with precision — it is not 'important' or 'the CEO asked for it,' it is 'the sprint fails without this.' Cover how to handle the political pressure to upgrade every item to Must Have, how to document and honor Won't Have decisions so they do not resurface in every planning meeting, and how to use MoSCoW in combination with effort estimation to build a realistic sprint commitment. Include guidance on how to differentiate MoSCoW from other prioritization frameworks (RICE, Kano, Value/Effort) and when each is most appropriate in the product development process. Provide a facilitation guide for a 90-minute sprint planning session that produces a clean MoSCoW-prioritized sprint backlog.","tags":["prioritization","agile","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-71","title":"Kano Model Feature Classification","prompt":"You are a product researcher who uses the Kano model to make prioritization decisions that maximize customer satisfaction. I have a list of 15 proposed features for a B2B analytics product and I want to classify them using the Kano model before committing to a roadmap. Walk me through the Kano model survey design and analysis process. Explain the functional and dysfunctional question pairs that reveal whether a feature is a Must-Be (expected baseline), a Performance feature (more is better), an Excitement feature (unexpected delight), or Indifferent (does not affect satisfaction). Provide the Kano survey template with example questions for three types of analytics features. Explain the analysis process: how to classify each feature from the survey response matrix, how to handle ambiguous results, and how to prioritize across Kano categories when resources are limited. Include guidance on how Kano classifications change over time as user expectations evolve — yesterday's excitement feature becoming today's must-be.","tags":["prioritization","research","frameworks"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-72","title":"Opportunity Scoring: Impact vs Effort Matrix","prompt":"You are a product manager who uses an impact-versus-effort matrix as a first-pass prioritization tool before applying more rigorous scoring frameworks. Walk me through how to run an effective impact-versus-effort mapping session with a cross-functional team. Explain how to define 'impact' in a way that is specific to the current strategic objective (not generic 'business value'), how to define 'effort' in a way that engineers can estimate quickly without detailed planning, and how to prevent the most common facilitation failures: scope creep in the discussion, anchoring effects where the first estimate influences all subsequent ones, and the tendency to rate everything as high impact. Provide a complete facilitation guide for a 90-minute session that produces a quadrant map with clear Quick Win, Major Project, Fill-In, and Thankless Task categories, and guidance on how to use the map to make actual prioritization decisions rather than treating it as a decoration.","tags":["prioritization","frameworks","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-73","title":"Saying No to Feature Requests: A PM's Framework","prompt":"You are a product manager who has mastered the art of saying no to feature requests in a way that maintains trust and credibility with customers, sales, and internal stakeholders. Walk me through a complete framework for evaluating and responding to feature requests. Cover the evaluation process: the five questions to ask about every feature request before forming a response (who has this problem, how many of them are there, what are they doing today, how does this fit the strategy, and what would we have to deprioritize to build it). Explain the three types of no: the 'not now' (we see the value but the timing is not right), the 'not this way' (the underlying need is real but this solution is not right), and the 'not ever' (this falls outside our strategy regardless of demand). For each type, provide exact language for the response to the requester — the words that communicate the decision clearly and respectfully without overpromising a future reversal. Include guidance for the hardest no: declining a request from a large customer who is threatening to churn.","tags":["prioritization","communication","stakeholders"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-74","title":"Feature Flags and Experimentation Infrastructure","prompt":"You are a platform product manager who has built the feature flag and experimentation infrastructure for a 200-person engineering organization. Walk me through the design and governance of a feature flag system that serves multiple purposes simultaneously: gradual rollouts, A/B tests, kill switches for risky changes, and operational flags for configuration management. Cover the flag lifecycle (creation, rollout, graduation to full release, and cleanup), the governance policies that prevent flag accumulation from becoming a maintenance burden (the maximum flag lifespan, the cleanup trigger conditions, and the ownership model), and the engineering requirements for a flag system that does not degrade application performance. Include the organizational rituals that make flag governance actually work in practice — the monthly flag review, the cleanup sprint, and the naming conventions that make a flag's purpose and owner clear from the name alone.","tags":["engineering","testing","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-75","title":"Executive Product Review Presentation","prompt":"You are a CPO who presents to a board of directors and C-suite quarterly. Walk me through how to design and deliver a product review presentation for an executive audience that has limited time, high authority, and mixed technical depth. Cover the structure: the one-slide context-setter (what was the strategic objective last quarter), the outcomes review (what metrics moved and by how much), the key learnings (what you learned that changes how you think about the product), the forward-looking plan (what you are committing to and why), and the asks (what you need from the executives to succeed). Explain the facilitation approach: how long to spend on each section, how to invite meaningful input without opening a free-for-all discussion, and how to handle the executive who derails the agenda with a pet topic. Include the pre-meeting preparation: what to send in advance, who to pre-brief, and what questions to prepare answers for so you are not surprised.","tags":["stakeholders","communication","leadership"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-76","title":"Cross-Functional Alignment Without Authority","prompt":"You are a product manager who operates without direct authority over engineering, design, data, or marketing — and yet consistently aligns these teams around shared goals. Walk me through the influence framework and operating practices that enable alignment without authority. Cover the trust-building behaviors that make cross-functional teammates want to collaborate (how to be a reliable partner to engineering, how to give design real influence in product decisions, how to keep marketing informed without making them feel like an afterthought), the alignment mechanisms that work at different organizational scales (the weekly cross-functional sync, the quarterly planning workshop, the async decision log), and the conflict resolution approaches for the most common cross-functional tensions (PM versus engineering on scope, PM versus design on trade-offs, PM versus sales on commitments). Include the three behaviors that damage cross-functional trust most quickly and how to avoid them even under deadline pressure.","tags":["stakeholders","leadership","teams"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-77","title":"Product Strategy Document for Company-Wide Alignment","prompt":"You are a VP of Product who writes the annual product strategy document that aligns 200 people across engineering, sales, marketing, and customer success around shared priorities. Walk me through how to write a product strategy document that actually changes behavior — not a document that gets filed after the all-hands and never referenced again. Cover the structure: the market context (what is changing in the world that makes this moment different), the strategic bets (the three to five things you are choosing to invest in and why), the deliberate trade-offs (what you are explicitly not doing and why the trade-off is correct), the success definition (how you will know in 12 months whether the strategy worked), and the resource allocation (how people and budget map to each strategic bet). Include the consultation and review process that builds buy-in before the document is finalized, and the rhythm for using the document throughout the year rather than letting it go stale after Q1.","tags":["strategy","communication","leadership"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-78","title":"Managing Up: Communicating with Your CEO","prompt":"You are a product leader who coaches PMs on how to build effective working relationships with CEOs and founders. Walk me through the framework for managing up as a PM. Cover the communication cadences that work for different CEO styles (the CEO who wants deep weekly dives versus the one who wants a brief async update, the CEO who is obsessed with customers versus the one who is obsessed with metrics), how to bring bad news early and in a way that is solution-oriented without being falsely optimistic, how to push back on a CEO's product idea while maintaining the relationship and preserving your credibility, and how to get the CEO to make a decision when they have been delaying for three weeks. Include specific language for the most challenging CEO conversations: disagreeing about product direction, explaining a missed target, and asking for resources the CEO is reluctant to allocate. Cover the behaviors that damage PM credibility with CEOs and how to repair trust after a breakdown.","tags":["stakeholders","leadership","communication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-79","title":"Sales Enablement for a New Product Feature","prompt":"You are a product manager who has reduced sales cycle length by investing in thorough sales enablement for every major feature release. Walk me through the complete sales enablement package for a new feature launch. Cover the components that salespeople actually use: the one-page feature summary (the pain it solves, the target customer profile, the value proposition in customer language, and the three discovery questions to identify a good candidate), the competitive differentiation talking points (specifically how the feature compares to the competitive alternatives the prospect has likely already evaluated), the objection handling guide (the five most common objections and the evidence-backed responses), and the demo script (the three-minute product demo that shows the feature's value without overwhelming the prospect with configuration). Include the sales readiness review process — how to certify that the sales team is ready to pitch the feature before the launch date, and what to do if they are not.","tags":["stakeholders","sales","launch"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-80","title":"Stakeholder Mapping and Influence Strategy","prompt":"You are a product manager at a large organization who treats stakeholder management as a product in itself. Walk me through a systematic stakeholder mapping and engagement process for a major product initiative. Cover the stakeholder identification process (who is affected by this decision, who has the power to block it, and who has expertise that should influence it), the stakeholder mapping matrix (power versus interest, and how to design a different engagement strategy for each quadrant), the influence tactics for each stakeholder type (how to engage a high-power, low-interest executive differently from a high-interest, low-power operational expert), and the early-warning system for detecting stakeholder misalignment before it becomes a project blocker. Include a stakeholder communication calendar that maps each stakeholder to the right touchpoint cadence and format, and the three most common stakeholder management mistakes that cause PM projects to stall or die.","tags":["stakeholders","leadership","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-81","title":"Product Launch Checklist and Coordination Framework","prompt":"You are a product manager who has led 15 product launches ranging from minor feature releases to major platform announcements. Walk me through the complete launch coordination framework for a significant feature release at a B2B SaaS company. Cover the four weeks before launch: the launch brief (what information every function needs to prepare), the readiness reviews (how to verify that product, engineering, QA, customer success, marketing, and legal are each ready), the communication cascade (the order in which customers, prospects, partners, and internal teams are informed), and the launch day war room (who is on call, what monitoring is in place, what the escalation protocol is for a critical bug found after launch). Include a 40-point launch checklist organized by function and timeline, the five most common launch failures and how to prevent each one, and the 48-hour post-launch review template that extracts learnings for the next launch.","tags":["launch","process","coordination"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:40:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-82","title":"Go-to-Market Strategy for a New Product Category","prompt":"You are a VP of Product who has launched products into new market categories where the buyer had to be educated about the problem before they could evaluate the solution. Walk me through a go-to-market strategy for a product that creates a new category rather than competing in an existing one. Cover the category design elements: how to name the category (the choice between a descriptive name that is clear and a proprietary name that you can own), how to define the 'before' state (the existing approach that the new category replaces), and how to build the category narrative in content and thought leadership before the product is ready to buy. Cover the launch sequencing: why to seed the category with five to ten champion customers before the public launch, how to design the initial pricing to optimize for market education rather than immediate revenue, and how to measure category creation success. Include the most common category creation mistakes and how to avoid the trap of creating a category no one joins.","tags":["launch","strategy","marketing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-83","title":"Beta Program Design and Management","prompt":"You are a product manager who has run beta programs that produced genuine product improvements rather than just early access marketing events. Walk me through the design of a structured beta program for a significant new feature. Cover the beta participant recruitment criteria (how to select users who have the problem the feature solves, are engaged enough to provide quality feedback, and are influential enough that their endorsement matters), the beta onboarding process (how to set expectations, provide support, and establish the feedback channel), the feedback collection system (the combination of in-product surveys, usage analytics, and periodic check-in calls that produces actionable insights), and the beta-to-GA transition (how to communicate the end of beta, how to handle features that beta users relied on but did not make the cut for GA, and how to convert beta users into advocates at launch). Include a beta health scoring system for tracking participant engagement and identifying at-risk beta relationships early.","tags":["launch","beta","research"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-84","title":"Post-Launch Review and Learning Extraction","prompt":"You are a product manager who treats post-launch reviews as the most valuable investment in future launch quality. Walk me through how to design and facilitate a post-launch review that produces genuine organizational learning rather than a blame-assignment exercise. Cover the preparation: what data to gather in the two weeks after launch (metric performance versus targets, customer feedback volume and themes, support ticket analysis, and team retrospective input), the facilitation approach (how to create psychological safety for honest assessment, how to ensure root cause analysis rather than surface-level symptom description), and the output format (the learning document that is specific enough to be actionable in the next launch, not a generic 'we should communicate better' summary). Include the five questions that unlock the most valuable insights in a post-launch review, and a template for a learning log that accumulates institutional knowledge about what makes launches succeed or fail.","tags":["launch","process","learning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-85","title":"Feature Announcement Copy and Release Notes","prompt":"You are a product marketer who turns dry feature releases into communications that customers actually read and act on. Walk me through the principles of effective feature announcement copy and release notes. Cover the audience segmentation (why the email to existing customers, the in-app announcement, the blog post, and the social media post each need different copy even if they are announcing the same feature), the narrative structure that works for feature announcements (the problem-solution-benefit arc and how to make it feel relevant rather than generic), and the UX writing principles for in-app announcements (how to be brief enough that users read it, specific enough that they understand what changed, and action-oriented enough that they try the feature). Include a transformation exercise: take three mediocre release notes and rewrite them into compelling announcements. End with a release notes template and a review checklist for evaluating announcement copy before it goes out.","tags":["launch","communication","writing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-86","title":"Launch Metrics and Success Measurement","prompt":"You are a data-focused product manager who defines launch success metrics before the launch, not after the results are in. Walk me through how to define a comprehensive launch measurement framework for a significant new feature. Cover the three categories of launch metrics: adoption metrics (how many users discover, try, and retain the feature in the first 30 days), quality metrics (error rates, performance benchmarks, and support ticket volume compared to pre-launch baseline), and business impact metrics (how the feature affects the product's core North Star Metric and revenue metrics). Explain how to set targets for each metric that are ambitious but grounded in comparable historical launches, how to build a launch monitoring dashboard that surfaces anomalies in real time, and how to define the launch success criteria that you will report to leadership at the 30-day and 90-day marks. Include guidance on distinguishing a slow launch from a failing launch and when to intervene.","tags":["launch","metrics","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-87","title":"Competitive Intelligence System for Product Teams","prompt":"You are a product manager who has built a competitive intelligence program that gives the product team a sustained information advantage. Walk me through how to build a lightweight but comprehensive competitive intelligence system for a team without a dedicated CI analyst. Cover the intelligence sources: the combination of public sources (product changelogs, job postings, pricing pages, G2 and Capterra reviews, conference talks, and podcast appearances) and customer intelligence sources (win-loss interviews, feature request patterns that reference competitors, and sales call recordings). Explain how to build a monitoring cadence that takes less than three hours per week, how to organize and distribute competitive intelligence so it is actually used by the team, and how to translate competitive signals into product decisions rather than just maintaining an awareness library. Include a one-page competitor profile template and a quarterly competitive review agenda.","tags":["competitive","research","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-88","title":"Win/Loss Analysis Program","prompt":"You are a product manager who has used win-loss analysis to identify and close the product gaps that were costing the company competitive deals. Walk me through the design of a win-loss analysis program for a B2B SaaS company closing 50 to 100 deals per quarter. Cover the data collection: the CRM fields to capture at deal close, the interview protocol for win and loss interviews (how to get buyers to participate, what to ask, and how to get past the polite 'price was the issue' answer to the real reasons), and the sample design (how many wins and losses per quarter provide statistically meaningful patterns versus noise). Cover the analysis process: how to code qualitative interview data, how to distinguish product gaps from pricing gaps from sales execution gaps, and how to build the report that makes product, marketing, and sales all want to read it. Include the five questions that are most likely to reveal the real reason a deal was won or lost.","tags":["competitive","research","sales"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-89","title":"SaaS Pricing Strategy Framework","prompt":"You are a pricing strategist who has redesigned pricing models for six B2B SaaS products and improved average contract value in each. Walk me through the complete pricing strategy framework for a B2B SaaS product. Cover the value metric selection (what the customer pays for — per seat, per usage, per outcome — and how to choose the metric that aligns your revenue with the value customers receive), the pricing model design (flat rate versus tiered versus usage-based versus hybrid), the tier architecture (how many tiers, what the plan differentiation logic is, and how to design plan limits that create natural upgrade triggers without frustrating customers), and the pricing level setting (how to use customer interviews, competitive benchmarking, and value-based analysis to set prices that are neither leaving money on the table nor blocking adoption). Include the five most common SaaS pricing mistakes and how each of them suppresses either adoption or revenue.","tags":["pricing","strategy","business"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:20:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-90","title":"Positioning Statement Development","prompt":"You are a product marketing strategist who has written positioning statements that successfully repositioned products in crowded markets. Walk me through the April Dunford positioning framework and how to apply it to a B2B SaaS product. Cover the five components of a complete positioning: the competitive alternatives (what customers actually compare you to, including doing nothing or using a spreadsheet), the unique attributes (the capabilities you have that the alternatives lack), the value for the customer (the business outcomes your unique attributes enable), the target customer (the specific characteristics of the customers who care most about those outcomes), and the market frame of reference (the category context that makes your value easy to understand). Explain how to run the positioning workshop with the leadership team, how to turn positioning into messaging for different channels, and how to test whether your positioning is working in the market. Include a worked example for a hypothetical product.","tags":["competitive","positioning","marketing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-91","title":"Pricing Page UX Optimization","prompt":"You are a conversion rate optimization specialist who has improved trial-to-paid conversion by optimizing pricing page design. Walk me through the UX design principles specific to pricing pages for B2B SaaS products. Cover the plan architecture display: how to present two to four plans in a way that guides the prospect toward the right plan without overwhelming them, the role of a 'recommended' plan highlight, and how to handle the enterprise tier that cannot be self-served. Cover the feature comparison table: what to include and what to omit (most comparison tables have too many rows, obscuring the key differentiators), how to handle binary versus tiered feature availability, and how to communicate limits in a way that feels fair rather than punitive. Include specific guidance on the elements that most reliably improve conversion: social proof placement, FAQ positioning, money-back guarantee messaging, and the annual versus monthly toggle. Provide a pricing page audit checklist with 20 items.","tags":["pricing","ux","conversion"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-92","title":"Price Increase Communication Strategy","prompt":"You are a product manager who has managed two successful price increases at SaaS companies without triggering significant churn. Walk me through the complete process of implementing and communicating a price increase to existing customers. Cover the analysis phase: how to model the revenue impact of different price increase magnitudes and churn scenarios, how to identify customer segments that are most and least price-sensitive, and how to determine the right grandfather period for existing customers. Cover the communication strategy: the timing (how far in advance to notify, the sequence of channels), the messaging framework (how to communicate the increase in a way that reinforces value rather than triggering a cancellation review), and the retention response plan (how to handle customers who respond by threatening to leave, and what offers you will and will not make). Include the specific communication templates for email, in-app notification, and the CSM conversation guide for customers who call in.","tags":["pricing","communication","retention"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-93","title":"Activation Rate Improvement Playbook","prompt":"You are a growth PM who has improved product activation rates from 30 percent to 65 percent in a 90-day sprint. I have a B2B SaaS product where 40 percent of new signups never complete the core setup flow and never experience the product's value. Walk me through the diagnostic and improvement process. Cover the diagnostic phase: how to use funnel analytics to find the specific step where the most users drop off, how to use session recordings to understand the behavior at the drop-off point, and how to use survey data to understand why users are leaving (is it confusion, distraction, or a realization that the product is not right for them?). Cover the improvement design phase: the three categories of activation improvements (reducing friction, increasing motivation, and restructuring the flow) and the specific tactics in each category. Include a prioritization framework for choosing which activation experiments to run first, and the measurement approach for tracking whether changes are working within a two-week sprint cycle.","tags":["onboarding","activation","growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-94","title":"User Onboarding for Enterprise Customers","prompt":"You are a product manager at a B2B SaaS company where your enterprise customers have complex organizational structures, multiple stakeholders involved in implementation, and a success team that needs to manage the process without being present for every step. Walk me through how to design an enterprise onboarding experience that is structured enough to ensure consistent outcomes but flexible enough to accommodate the variation in how different enterprise customers are organized. Cover the onboarding design elements: the admin setup flow (IT and security configuration), the workspace configuration flow (the business administrator who sets up teams, templates, and integrations), the end-user training experience (how to onboard 50 to 500 users to a new tool efficiently), and the milestone tracking system (how to give both the customer and your CS team visibility into onboarding progress). Include a 90-day onboarding plan template and the metrics that predict whether an enterprise customer will reach full adoption or stall.","tags":["onboarding","enterprise","customer-success"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-95","title":"In-App Guidance System Design","prompt":"You are a product designer who has designed in-app guidance systems that improve feature discovery and reduce support volume without annoying users. Walk me through the design of a comprehensive in-app guidance system for a complex B2B product. Cover the guidance type taxonomy: product tours (for first-time users who need a high-level orientation), interactive walkthroughs (for new features being introduced to existing users), contextual tooltips (for specific UI elements that are not self-explanatory), empty state guidance (for the cold-start problem), and proactive tips (for features users have never used but that are relevant to their role). For each type, explain the trigger conditions (when to show it), the dismissal and persistence logic (how to respect users who have already seen it), and the success measurement (how to know whether the guidance is helping users or just adding noise). Include a guidance system audit checklist for evaluating whether an existing system is working or needs to be simplified.","tags":["onboarding","ux","engagement"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-96","title":"Churn Prevention: Identifying and Rescuing At-Risk Users","prompt":"You are a retention-focused product manager who has built proactive churn prevention programs using product usage data. Walk me through the design of a churn prediction and prevention system for a B2B SaaS product with a monthly subscription model. Cover the predictive signal identification: the combination of usage behaviors (login frequency decline, feature usage breadth shrinkage, team size contraction) and relationship signals (support tickets without resolution, renewal conversation avoidance) that together predict churn 30 to 60 days in advance with meaningful accuracy. Cover the intervention design: the in-product interventions (re-engagement campaigns, feature discovery prompts, and onboarding re-entry for users who never fully activated) and the human interventions (the CS playbook for different risk signals and risk magnitudes). Include the measurement framework for the intervention program — how to evaluate whether interventions are causing retention improvement or just correlating with it — and the feedback loop for improving signal accuracy over time.","tags":["retention","churn","analytics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-97","title":"Notification and Email Strategy for Engagement","prompt":"You are a product manager who has designed notification strategies that improve engagement without increasing unsubscribe rates. Walk me through the design of a comprehensive notification and email strategy for a B2B SaaS product. Cover the notification taxonomy: real-time activity notifications (someone commented on your document), digest notifications (a weekly summary of activity), milestone notifications (you reached X), and re-engagement notifications (you have not logged in for two weeks). For each type, explain the optimal timing, the channel (in-app, email, push, or Slack), the content principles, and the personalization logic that makes the notification relevant rather than generic. Cover the preference management design: how to give users meaningful control over notifications without presenting an overwhelming settings page, and how to use preference data to improve notification relevance. Include a notification audit checklist and the metrics that distinguish a healthy notification strategy from one that is burning user goodwill.","tags":["engagement","email","retention"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-98","title":"Feature Request Analysis and Response","prompt":"You are a senior product manager with expertise in turning ambiguous customer requests into clear product decisions. I will share a feature request or user feedback with you. Your task is to help me analyze it thoroughly and decide how to respond. For the input provided, do the following: identify the surface request (what the user is literally asking for) versus the underlying job to be done (what they are actually trying to accomplish), assess the likely size of the user population with this need based on any signals in the request, evaluate three possible solutions ranging from minimal to comprehensive and assess the trade-offs of each, recommend a response to the user that is honest and specific about whether and how this might be addressed, and draft a one-paragraph internal summary I can use to log this request in the product backlog with enough context for a future PM to understand the decision without interviewing me.\n\n[Paste your feature request or user feedback]","tags":["requirements","research","communication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"prod-99","title":"Product Strategy Diagnosis and Recommendations","prompt":"You are a product strategy consultant who has assessed over 40 B2B SaaS products and identified the strategic patterns that separate high-growth products from stalled ones. I will describe my product and the challenge I am facing. Your task is to provide a thorough strategic diagnosis and a prioritized set of recommendations. Structure your response as follows: a restatement of the core strategic challenge in your own words to confirm understanding, an assessment of the most likely root causes (distinguish between market fit issues, product quality issues, go-to-market issues, and organizational issues), three to five specific strategic recommendations ordered by expected impact and implementation difficulty, the key assumptions in each recommendation and how to test them quickly, and the one thing I should stop doing because it is consuming resources without contributing to the core strategic challenge.\n\n[Describe your product and current challenge]","tags":["strategy","diagnosis","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"prod-100","title":"UX Review and Improvement Recommendations","prompt":"You are a senior UX designer and product thinker who has reviewed hundreds of B2B SaaS interfaces and identified the usability patterns that most reliably improve task completion and user satisfaction. I will describe a specific UX flow or interface. Your task is to provide a structured UX review with actionable recommendations. Cover: the primary usability issues (problems that prevent users from completing their goal), the secondary quality issues (friction points that do not block completion but create unnecessary effort or confusion), the UX writing issues (labels, instructions, and error messages that could be clearer), the accessibility concerns (issues that would affect users relying on keyboard navigation or assistive technology), and a prioritized improvement plan that distinguishes quick wins (fixes that take less than a day) from structural improvements (changes that require design and development investment). For each issue, be specific about why it is a problem, who it affects most, and what the improved version should look like.\n\n[Describe the UX flow or interface you want reviewed]","tags":["ux","design","review"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:15:00Z","featured":true}

];
  __exports.productPrompts = productPrompts;
})();
